---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Toc from '../components/Toc.astro';
import {withBase} from '../lib/paths';

const toc = [
	{id: 'steps', label: 'What it does'},
	{id: 'domain', label: 'Domain objects'},
	{id: 'parse', label: 'Parsing'},
	{id: 'errors', label: 'Error codes'},
	{id: 'http', label: 'Paid HTTP'},
	{id: 'tui', label: 'TUI'},
];
---

<DocsLayout
	title="OSINT Scanner"
	eyebrow="[ 04 · RECON ]"
	description="Map a deployed contract’s attack surface from WASM alone — no source, no ABI file, no cooperation from the author."
>
	<Toc items={toc} />

	<h2 id="steps">What it does</h2>
	<p>Given only a Contract ID, the scanner:</p>
	<ol>
		<li>
			Fetches WASM via read-only RPC (<code>getContractWasmByContractId</code>) — nothing
			broadcast.
		</li>
		<li>Decodes the <code>contractspecv0</code> custom section from the binary.</li>
		<li>Builds a registry of user-defined types (structs, unions, enums).</li>
		<li>Renders every function: name, parameter types, return presence.</li>
	</ol>
	<p>
		This mirrors how an external attacker approaches a closed target: only what the chain
		publishes.
	</p>

	<h2 id="domain">Domain objects</h2>
	<pre><code>ScanResult {'{'}
  contractId: string
  bytecodeSize: number
  functions: ScannedFunction[]   // name, params[], hasReturn
  udtRegistry: UdtRegistry       // Map of UDT definitions
{'}'}

ScannedFunction {'{'}
  name: string
  params: {'{'} name: string; type: ScSpecTypeDef {'}'}[]
  hasReturn: boolean             // domain fact; UI chooses labels
{'}'}</code></pre>

	<p>
		<code>ScanResult</code> is the bridge into Chaos Monkey. It is <strong>not</strong> the public
		JSON schema — the UdtRegistry stays a Map; public reports project human-readable type names
		via <code>typeName()</code>.
	</p>

	<h2 id="parse">Parsing notes</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<code>parseContractSpecSection()</code> is pure and network-free — unit-tested with
			synthetic XDR buffers.
		</li>
		<li>
			Alignment-tolerant decode: brute-force 4-byte aligned lengths until an
			<code>ScSpecEntry</code> parses, then advance the cursor.
		</li>
		<li>
			<code>isValidContractId</code> shared by TUI + CLI:
			<code>{'/^C[A-Z0-9]{55}$/'}</code>.
		</li>
		<li>
			UDT resolution (later, in Chaos Monkey) has depth limits and cycle detection; unknown
			types → skip generation, never invent values.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="errors">Error codes</h2>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th>Code</th>
				<th>When</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td><code>CONTRACT_NOT_FOUND</code></td>
				<td>No WASM for ID on Testnet RPC</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>XDR_ALIGN_FAILURE</code></td>
				<td>contractspec section could not be parsed</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>RPC_UNAVAILABLE</code></td>
				<td>Transport / endpoint failure</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>UNKNOWN</code></td>
				<td>Catch-all</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		Presentation layers must switch on <code>ScanError.code</code>, never parse
		<code>message</code> text. Recovery tips →
		<a href={withBase('/troubleshooting')}>Troubleshooting</a>.
	</p>

	<Callout type="tip" title="Paid HTTP surface">
		<p>
			<code>kuyfi-server</code> exposes only this read-only scanner over
			<code>GET /scan/:contractId</code> (x402). Details →
			<a href={withBase('/paid-api')}>Paid scan API</a>.
		</p>
	</Callout>

	<h2 id="tui">TUI</h2>
	<p>
		Home → <strong>[1] OSINT Scanner</strong>. Paste a Contract ID, watch the fetch, inspect
		the attack-surface table. The last successful scan can feed Chaos Monkey in the same
		session (<code>lastScanResult</code> in <code>app.tsx</code>).
	</p>
</DocsLayout>
